+    "findings": "<p>The authors identified several synthetic polymers as well as cellulose in container extracts. Their pixel-based counting definition limits comparison with particle-by-particle counts. The library uses this paper to explain method differences and does not reproduce its abundance estimates.</p><p>The experiment recovered material from the tested containers using water-contact procedures before filtering the extracts for infrared examination. The nylon samples received a different preparation from the other container samples. Glass-beaker controls were treated alongside the samples. These conditions belong with the findings; they do not describe all containers or every use of a container with an actual meal. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules27092646\">Source: PDF p. 3, sections 2.2-2.3</a>.</p><p>The analysis compared recorded spectra with reference spectra at image-pixel locations. The authors state that the number of pixels represented the number of microplastics in their analysis. Chemical assignment of image locations can identify polymer signals, but that counting definition is not the same as reconstructing and counting separate physical particles. The library therefore retains the method description without transferring the paper's abundance estimates into a common particle-release comparison. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules27092646\">Source: PDF p. 4, section 2.4 Data Analysis</a>.</p><p>The reported material assignments include synthetic polymers and cellulose. The text excludes cellulose in one abundance description while including it in a later composition description, so the denominator needs attention before percentages are reused. The authors also discuss possible sources of material other than the original container. These observations support a cautious reading of composition and source attribution, rather than treating every identified object as newly shed plastic from the tested package. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules27092646\">Source: PDF p. 4, section 3.1 Abundances of Microplastics</a>.</p>",
