+    "findings": "<p>Raman identification and microscopy provide complementary composition, size and count information; contamination controls remain necessary.</p><p>The method states a lowest reliably detected particle size of {{claim:s-5a6c89659a69-lower-size}}. That is a method-scope value, not a safe exposure threshold.</p><p>The supplied procedure uses visual microscopy for counts and Raman spectroscopy for chemical identification in treated drinking water. Samples are separated into size fractions before examination. A representative subset of counted particles is selected for spectroscopy, measured and photographed, and compared with reference spectra. The proportion confirmed as plastic is then used in estimating the microplastic count. The final estimate should therefore not be described as individual chemical confirmation of every object originally seen. <a href=\"https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/drinking_water/certlic/drinkingwater/documents/microplastics/swb-mp2-rev1.pdf\">Source: PDF pp. 2-3, sections 1.0 and 2.0</a>.</p><p>The scope states that sample collection protocols are outside this procedure and that extraction below its validated size fraction has not been validated by this method. It also distinguishes a minimum detectable amount from the sampled water volume and requires laboratories to establish their own values. These statements keep the method-specific measurement capability separate from a universal claim about every Raman instrument or the presence of still smaller particles. <a href=\"https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/drinking_water/certlic/drinkingwater/documents/microplastics/swb-mp2-rev1.pdf\">Source: PDF p. 2, sections 1.2-1.5</a>.</p><p>The procedure requires trained analysts to demonstrate acceptable performance. Its definitions distinguish air blanks, field blanks and laboratory reagent blanks because they address contamination introduced at different stages. Fortified blanks instead help evaluate recovery and analytical performance with added particles. A reliable interpretation reads the reported result alongside these controls, the particle-selection process and the implemented method. The procedure does not itself establish a clinical effect or a safe drinking-water concentration. <a href=\"https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/drinking_water/certlic/drinkingwater/documents/microplastics/swb-mp2-rev1.pdf\">Source: PDF pp. 3-4, sections 1.6 and 3.0</a>.</p>",
