+    "findings": "<p>The review evaluates analytical approaches alongside sampling, size, polymer and reporting considerations. Method suitability depends on the measurement question and matrix.</p><p>The report was prepared to support the development of a drinking-water measurement methodology. It reviews how samples were collected, how particles were separated and identified, and which descriptors laboratories could report. Its comparison includes fluorescence microscopy, vibrational microspectroscopy and thermal methods coupled to mass spectrometry. These techniques answer different measurement questions: counting chemically identified objects and measuring polymer mass are distinct outcomes. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2760/109944\">Source: PDF pp. 6 and 8, Abstract and Executive summary; printed pp. 4 and 6</a>.</p><p>The conclusions explain why a method cannot be selected from instrument sensitivity alone. The measurement metric, size range, chemical specificity, accuracy and precision need to fit the monitoring question. Laboratory throughput and cost also affect whether a procedure can be used routinely. The report describes a practical choice among capabilities and limitations, rather than identifying a universally superior instrument for every sample. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2760/109944\">Source: PDF p. 58, section 9 Summary and conclusions; printed p. 56</a>.</p><p>Background contamination is part of that comparison. Material introduced during handling can contribute substantially when the amount collected from water is small. The review also notes that filters and the subsequent analytical workflow determine which particles remain available for measurement. A published concentration therefore needs its sampling and size context before comparison with another study. The source is a technical review of available evidence, not a new survey of every drinking-water supply or a direct measurement of consumer health effects. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2760/109944\">Source: PDF pp. 19 and 58, sections 2.4.5 and 9; printed pp. 17 and 56</a>.</p>",
