+    "findings": "<p>Blank characterization, recovery checks, spectral identification, sizing and clear reporting determine what an analytical result can support.</p><p>This practical report explains how the drinking-water methodology can be implemented, using feasibility work and equipment examples from the JRC laboratory. It follows the earlier technical review but has a different role: showing the reasoning behind sampling, pretreatment, instrumental analysis and reporting choices. The laboratory examples illustrate implementation and limitations; they do not turn a named instrument into a universal method for every water or product. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2760/1240697\">Source: PDF pp. 5 and 7, Abstract and Executive Summary; printed pp. 2 and 4</a>.</p><p>Sampling and treatment affect what reaches the instrument. The report distinguishes collection filters from blank filters and explains how different particle fractions can need different analytical arrangements. Nonplastic matter can overload a filter, hide particles or interfere with chemical identification. Pretreatment may therefore be necessary, while transfer between supports can introduce particle loss. Direct analysis on a compatible collection filter can avoid that transfer, but compatibility depends on the sample and analytical method. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2760/1240697\">Source: PDF pp. 20-21, sections 3.1-3.2; printed pp. 17-18</a>.</p><p>The adaptability discussion is conditional. Water with more suspended material and solid matrices can require substantial changes to sampling or preparation. The report also distinguishes particle-number data from the mass-based information needed for other applications. Those quantities cannot be assumed interchangeable. Its discussion of possible extensions is a starting point for evaluation, rather than evidence that the drinking-water procedure has already been validated for food, tissue, wastewater sludge or every source of environmental particles. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2760/1240697\">Source: PDF pp. 35-36, sections 4-5; printed pp. 32-33</a>.</p>",
