+    "findings": "<p>The Panel called for improved, standardized analytical methods, demonstrated quality assurance and better occurrence and toxicity data. This is a scientific assessment, distinct from a legal product limit.</p><p>The statement reviews the evidence available to the Panel at the time of its assessment, with particular attention to seafood. Its summary separates removing biological material, detecting and counting objects, and characterizing their chemical identity. It notes that some preparation procedures can also damage plastics. A count cannot be interpreted fully without knowing what survived preparation and how the remaining objects were assigned to plastic. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2903/j.efsa.2016.4501\">Source: PDF pp. 1 and 3, Abstract and Summary</a>.</p><p>The Panel identifies limited occurrence data and incomplete quality-assurance reporting. Some studies did not clearly describe how contamination from air or equipment was controlled, or how plastic identity was determined. It also notes differences in the units used for seafood results. The scientific question is therefore broader than whether an object was seen: sampling, material identification, contamination controls and measurement basis all affect the strength of an occurrence finding. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2903/j.efsa.2016.4501\">Source: PDF pp. 3 and 23, Summary and section 4.1.1 Data gaps</a>.</p><p>The conclusions and recommendations identify missing information on food processing, particle fate and toxicity relevant to human risk assessment. They call for standardized analytical methods, demonstrated quality assurance and better occurrence and toxicological evidence. These are conclusions of the supplied dated statement. They are not a description of every later study, a current worldwide legal register or a category-specific safe particle count. The source remains useful for understanding the assessment questions and gaps it actually addressed. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2903/j.efsa.2016.4501\">Source: PDF pp. 22-24, sections 3.9, 4 and 5</a>.</p>",
