+    "findings": "<p>The authors could not extrapolate a human-health-based threshold from the reviewed evidence. They distinguished a monitoring screening approach from a demonstrated safe exposure level.</p><p>The paper reports an expert workshop and a critical appraisal of mammalian hazard studies. The participants screened studies for particle characterization, experimental design and usefulness for risk assessment. Oral exposure studies in living mammals were prioritized for the workshop assessment. The authors explain that extrapolation from cell experiments to whole organisms was not established for this particulate exposure question, so the different experimental systems were not treated as interchangeable evidence. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s43591-022-00030-6\">Source: PDF pp. 1-3, Abstract and Method: Data collection and screening</a>.</p><p>The assessment found important weaknesses in the evidence available for deriving a human-health threshold. Laboratory exposures commonly used uniform particles from a limited polymer type, whereas environmental microplastics differ in composition and physical properties. The authors could not reliably extend those experiments into a demonstrated threshold for the heterogeneous particles encountered through drinking water. This is a limitation of the inference, rather than a finding that every possible exposure has the same effect. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s43591-022-00030-6\">Source: PDF pp. 1-2, Abstract</a>.</p><p>The proposed screening approach was intended to inform monitoring and future research. It is distinct from a concentration shown to be safe for people and from an observed concentration in a particular product. The paper calls for stronger toxicity studies and better exposure information to support later assessment. Reading its screening calculations as a clinical cutoff would give them an authority and application the authors did not establish. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s43591-022-00030-6\">Source: PDF pp. 1-3, Abstract and Introduction</a>.</p>",
